{
  "id": "mut-024",
  "pattern": "mutable-state",
  "difficulty_rating": 1625,
  "explanation": "makeConfigBuggy doesn't build a new object at all — it mutates and returns the single module-level `shared` object every time. So every entry in `configs` is the same object under three different names, not three independent configs. Once the loop applies the third override ({ retries: 8 }), that change is visible through every earlier reference too, so configs[0].retries, configs[1].retries, and configs[2].retries all read 8 — even though configs[0] was built from an override that only ever mentioned retries: 5.",
  "prompt": "makeConfigBuggy is meant to build an independent config object per call. Step through buildConfigs and predict each config's final `retries` value.",
  "language": "javascript",
  "snippet": "const shared = { retries: 3, timeout: 1000 };\nfunction makeConfigBuggy(overrides) {\n  Object.assign(shared, overrides);\n  return shared;\n}\nfunction buildConfigs(overrideList) {\n  const configs = [];\n  for (let i = 0; i < overrideList.length; i++) {\n    configs.push(makeConfigBuggy(overrideList[i]));\n  }\n  return configs;\n}\nconst configs = buildConfigs([{ retries: 5 }, { timeout: 2000 }, { retries: 8 }]);\nconsole.log(configs[0].retries, configs[1].retries, configs[2].retries);",
  "interaction": "scrubber",
